      "completion_tokens": 991,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance with the molecular formula **H₂O**, consisting of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. It is a fundamental component of life and exists in three primary states: **liquid** (common at room temperature), **solid** (ice, when frozen), and **gas** (water vapor, when heated). \n\n### Key Characteristics:\n1. **Chemical Structure**: Water molecules are **polar**, with oxygen having a slight negative charge and hydrogens a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water to form **hydrogen bonds**, which influence its unique properties, such as high surface tension, cohesion, and adhesion.\n\n2. **Physical Properties**:\n   - **High specific heat capacity**: It absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and environments.\n   - **Universal solvent**: It dissolves many substances, facilitating chemical reactions in biological systems and ecosystems.\n   - **Anomalous expansion**: It expands when freezing, making ice less dense than liquid water (which is why ice floats).\n\n3. **Biological Importance**:\n   - Essential for **all known forms of life**, making up 60–70% of the human body. It acts as a **solvent**, **transport medium**, and **reactant** in metabolic processes.\n   - Crucial for **photosynthesis** in plants and **temperature regulation** in animals.\n\n4. **Environmental Role**:\n   - Part of the **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation), sustaining ecosystems and weather patterns.\n   - A habitat for aquatic life and a resource for agriculture, industry, and human consumption.\n\n5. **Unique Properties**:\n   - **Cohesion** (molecules sticking together) and **adhesion** (molecules sticking to surfaces) enable capillary action in plants.\n   - **High heat of vaporization**, which aids in cooling through evaporation (e.g., sweating).\n\nIn summary, water is a vital, versatile substance with properties that make it indispensable for life, environmental stability, and countless natural and industrial processes."
